DISCLOSURE 1, THE APPROVED SPEC CONTRADICTED ITSELF AND THE AUTHORITY APPROVED IT. Recorded as F75. The signature-key Constraint said the assertions-mode refusal and the gate-verb refusal are separate reds; AC-HEAD-RED allows one key per criterion so both sit under as-vocabulary as one; and AC-HEAD-RED's own failure conditions forbid the pair that obeying the Constraint would create, because at HEAD both refusals produce the same observed failure. The executor followed AC-HEAD-RED and implemented the Constraint's intent as independently failable tests rather than extra ledger events. The durable rule: red counts live in exactly one place, and any other section may require independent observability without saying how many fail events that implies.

DISCLOSURE 2, A HEAD CLAIM THE PLAN AND THE AUTHORITY BOTH ASSERTED WITHOUT MEASURING. Recorded as F76. Both said an undeclared as would fail as an unknown flag. It does not: the CLI accepts an unrecognised argument, emits no diagnostic naming it, and returns a normal report. Verified by the authority afterwards, and note the trap - the exit code was 1 from unrelated lint errors, so checking status alone would have confirmed the wrong conclusion. Consequence beyond this bundle: a transposed letter in assertions runs the default mode and reports clean, across every selected-assertion command in the lifecycle. The irony is exact, since this bundle implements the step whose normative text is that silence must not read as will pass. Not fixed here; it belongs to the shared argument layer and tightening it is a compatibility break.

DISCLOSURE 3, A DEFECT REPAIRED BY A SUBAGENT DURING THE CLOSE, NOT BY THE AUTHORITY. The applied-plan-missing overlay carried a tautologically false conjunct, asStatus === applied &amp;&amp; asStatus !== applied, so that preview could never fire through that branch. The intended predicate tests the plan's previewed status, which is not always the requested status: overlayChangeStatus leaves a rootless plan.xml untouched, which is exactly the shape the real rule fires on. Repaired to compare the overlaid plan's own status, proved by a case that fails against the old expression and passes against the fix, with a paired control that passes both ways so the repair adds no false positive. No existing test was weakened; two were added. THE AUTHORITY DELEGATED THIS RATHER THAN WRITING IT, which is the standing rule after the user challenged the same authority writing production code at the previous close.

DISCLOSURE 4, A LATENT HONESTY GAP IN THE HONESTY FEATURE. Recorded as F77. The coverage line's class membership is a hand-written conditional over requested status and plan existence, not an instrumentation of which evaluators ran. AC-AS-ABSENCE-REPORT's requirement that N and M be derived and not a frozen pair IS met - the same status yields different M depending on real project state, which no status table could produce, and the tests pin that. The residual risk is that a fourth impure class would not be mentioned by the conditional, M would silently under-count, and the tool would under-report its own blindness. No test asserts the taxonomy is complete, so such a class is invisible rather than red. The executor disclosed the gap rather than claiming instrumentation it had not built. Owed before any fourth class is introduced.

DISCLOSURE 5, THE STEP COULD NOT BE BUILT AS WRITTEN. Fourth roadmap claim in this phase to fail construction, after P1.11's permanently blocked, P1.4's zero-depth globstar and P1.8's delayed IMPL=0. Step 6 demanded a pure preview of target and final while forbidding preview of anything impure, and validateAssertions is the impure evaluator of exactly those modes. It also named the wrong three states: approve is fully previewable, target and final cannot be previewed at all, and the state where the absence count is genuinely non-zero is applied, which the sentence never mentions. Resolved as a ChangeStatus overlay of the artifact-pure rules with typed absence for the rest.

DISCLOSURE 6, A CITATION DEFECT IN A NORMATIVE BLOCK. Recorded as F74 and repaired in the roadmap. Step 6 cited D5 for the typed-absence idiom; this plan's D5 is the separator rule and the idiom belongs to RM-AGENT-RELIABILITY D5 in the archive. Both plans number D1 into the teens, so every bare D-number in this repository is ambiguous. The approved spec still carries the bare citation at spec.xml:192 and was left immutable; the plan cites correctly. An audit of the remaining bare citations is owed.

DISCLOSURE 7. decisions.md was written during the open window - F74 and F75 before execution, F76 and F77 mid-window, each in its own commit ahead of the implementation commit. decisions.md is outside every bundle's ObservedWriteScope by design, and the scope audit reads working-tree porcelain, so committing removed those edits from the audit's view rather than clearing them.

DISCLOSURE 8, NAMES THE PLAN INVENTED. evaluateApplyGateArtifact is a plan-level identifier, not a spec symbol; the spec required only a distinct artifact-pure entry point. The Baseline MustNotContain on it flipped as planned. A later reader preferring another name should treat that as a plan defect to surface, never as a licence to edit a BaselineAssertion. Pins re-measured rather than frozen: D13 top-level keys still exact and gain only the already-declared changeId under as; boolean sites still 25; the four absence codes unmoved; token-accounting unmoved.</Verdict></Verdicts><Decisions><Decision gate="apply" decision="permit"><Requirement id="plan-present" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">status=approved</Requirement><Requirement id="no-unresolved-satisfied-ac-clarification" required="true" present="true" blocking="false" /><Requirement id="review-verdict" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">outcome=pass</Requirement></Decision><Decision gate="archive" decision="permit"><Requirement id="no-open-epoch" required="true" present="true" blocking="false">run/ empty</Requirement></Decision></Decisions></C-AS-STATE></NgraceRunLedger>
